# name: test/verify_mars_off_same_catalog.test
# description: same-catalog read+write with MARS OFF — the shape that is broken on every no-MARS engine
# group: [fabricator]

require fabricator

require-env MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS PINS, AND WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL
#
# A statement that reads and writes the SAME catalog marks its scan. A marked scan used to be DRAINED
# unconditionally, which PINS it onto the transaction's connection — the same connection the bulk load
# runs on. The bulk's consumer calls SqlBulkCopy.WriteToServer as soon as it starts, so the scan's
# reader and the load are concurrent BY CONSTRUCTION. With MARS they coexist. Without it they cannot.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-10 before the fix: this box shape failed **0 of 8 runs**, and Fabric (which has no
# MARS at all) failed 4 of 4 — as "does not support MultipleActiveResultSets", "already an open
# DataReader", or a 30 s Execution Timeout that killed the whole transaction. Full grid:
# docs/transactions.md §5.6a.
#
# ⚠ THE BUG WAS ONLY EVER SEEN ON FABRIC, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS FILE. Box hides it because
# MARS is ON by default there, so every existing suite passes while the shape is broken on the engine
# most users deploy to. `SET mssql_mars='false'` reproduces it on box in under a second, which is what
# makes it gateable at all — and its absence is why it survived for months.
#
# ⚠ IT WAS ALSO FLAKY, NOT DEAD: roughly one run in a dozen succeeded. A single green sample is
# therefore worthless here — the ONLY reason these sections can assert `ok` is that the fix makes them
# deterministic (8/8 after, 0/8 before). If one of them ever goes intermittent, treat that as the bug
# returning, not as a flaky test.
#
# The fix: `mssql_materialize` DEFAULTS TO MARS. On a MARS engine a marked scan is still drained and
# pinned (§1, read-your-writes preserved); without MARS it takes the snapshot-read route — pooled, at
# SNAPSHOT — so it never shares the connection with the load. The cost is read-your-writes on the
# scanned table, accepted deliberately for a bulk path and pinned in §4.

# ⚠ THE MODE IS A PER-CATALOG **ATTACH OPTION** HERE, AND IT HAS TO BE — a `SET` cannot express this suite
# any more. Until 2026-08-11 MARS was frozen per catalog at its first connect, so `SET` before each ATTACH
# was how you got two catalogs on different modes; now it is resolved PER CONNECTION from the session
# (`verify_mars_dynamic`), under which those two attaches would be IDENTICAL and whichever value the session
# held last would govern both. Written the old way, this suite's §1 "MARS ON control" silently ran with MARS
# OFF — caught by §0, which is exactly what that section exists for.
#
# The `mars` ATTACH option was added with that change to keep the per-catalog form expressible. No `SET`
# appears in this suite: a SET outranks the option (as it does for materialize / read_isolation), so one
# would flatten both catalogs again.
statement ok
ATTACH '${MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN}' AS m_on (TYPE fabricator, mars 'auto');

statement ok
ATTACH '${MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN}' AS m_off (TYPE fabricator, mars 'false');

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §0  THE SETTING ACTUALLY REACHED EACH CATALOG — the assertion this suite spent its life without.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ⚠ EVERY SECTION BELOW CAN PASS VACUOUSLY IF `SET mssql_mars='false'` DID NOT REACH `m_off`, because a
# MARS-ON catalog satisfies the "it works" assertions too — the header above already warns that writing the
# SET/ATTACH order wrong "silently produced a MARS-ON catalog and a vacuously passing suite", and until
# `mars_enabled` was added to fabricator_server_info() there was NO observable that could tell.
#
# It matters more since ABI v69 made settings SESSION-scoped, because "did my SET reach this catalog?" now
# has a second way to go wrong (the wrong session, not just the wrong order). ⚠ It does NOT today —
# `open_catalog` merely constructs the catalog, and the metadata calls that follow establish the session
# themselves — but that was established with a mutant rather than assumed, and this section is what would
# catch it changing.
#
# `supports_mars` is the SERVER's capability and stays true on box for both — which is exactly why it
# cannot serve as this check, and why the pair is asserted together.

query TT
SELECT property, value FROM fabricator_server_info('m_on')
WHERE property IN ('supports_mars', 'mars_enabled') ORDER BY property;
----
mars_enabled	true
supports_mars	true

query TT
SELECT property, value FROM fabricator_server_info('m_off')
WHERE property IN ('supports_mars', 'mars_enabled') ORDER BY property;
----
mars_enabled	false
supports_mars	true

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §1  MARS ON — the shipped box behaviour. POSITIVE CONTROL for everything below.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Without this section §2–§4 could pass equally if same-catalog read+write had simply stopped working,
# or if the tables had stopped being created.

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_src AS SELECT i AS a, ('v' || i::VARCHAR) AS b FROM range(200) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_dst AS SELECT a, b FROM m_on.dbo.mo_src;

query II
SELECT count(*), sum(a) FROM m_on.dbo.mo_dst;
----
200	19900

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_on.dbo.mo_dst SELECT a, b FROM m_on.dbo.mo_src;

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_on.dbo.mo_dst;
----
400

# READ-YOUR-WRITES on a MARS engine: the drain pins the scan inside the transaction, so the CTAS sees
# rows this transaction has not committed yet. This is what §4 gives up, and pinning it here is what
# makes that trade visible rather than accidental.
statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_ryw AS SELECT i AS a FROM range(10) t(i);

statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_on.dbo.mo_ryw SELECT 100 + i FROM range(5) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_ryw_copy AS SELECT a FROM m_on.dbo.mo_ryw;

statement ok
COMMIT;

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_on.dbo.mo_ryw_copy;
----
15

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §2  MARS OFF, INSERT — THE REGRESSION GATE. This is the 0-of-8 shape.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_src AS SELECT i AS a, ('v' || i::VARCHAR) AS b FROM range(200) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_dst AS SELECT i AS a, ('v' || i::VARCHAR) AS b FROM range(1) t(i) WHERE false;

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mf_dst SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mf_src;

# The COUNT is the assertion that matters: the pre-fix failure landed ZERO rows while the statement
# reported an error, so a test that only checked "no error" would still have caught it — but one that
# only checked the count would not have caught a partial load. Both, then.
query II
SELECT count(*), sum(a) FROM m_off.dbo.mf_dst;
----
200	19900

# The values, not just the cardinality — a pooled scan must not reorder or drop columns.
query I
SELECT b FROM m_off.dbo.mf_dst WHERE a = 137;
----
v137

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §3  MARS OFF, CTAS — a SEPARATE shape, not covered by §2
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ⚠ CTAS and INSERT route their marked scan DIFFERENTLY (measured: on Fabric the CTAS's scan logged
# `pooled` and the INSERT's `pinned` under identical settings), because the routing turns on whether
# the transaction's connection exists YET when the scan starts and the two operators initialise their
# sink in a different order. So neither section covers the other.

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ctas AS SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mf_src;

query II
SELECT count(*), sum(a) FROM m_off.dbo.mf_ctas;
----
200	19900

# ... and inside an EXPLICIT transaction that has already written, which is where the 30 s timeout hit.
statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mf_src VALUES (999, 'sentinel');

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ctas2 AS SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mf_src;

statement ok
COMMIT;

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mf_src;
----
201

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §4  MARS OFF — READ-YOUR-WRITES IS ABSENT. The accepted cost, pinned.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Without MARS the marked scan reads a committed snapshot, so a table this transaction has just
# written is seen at its COMMITTED state. That is deliberate (a bulk path moves bytes; it does not
# need to observe its own uncommitted rows) and it is the direct trade for §2 working at all — every
# configuration that WOULD deliver read-your-writes here is one that deadlocks.
#
# It is pinned rather than left implicit so that regaining it — or losing it on a MARS engine, where
# §1 asserts it is still there — has to be a deliberate change with a failing test attached.

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ryw AS SELECT i AS a FROM range(10) t(i);

statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mf_ryw SELECT 100 + i FROM range(5) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ryw_copy AS SELECT a FROM m_off.dbo.mf_ryw;

statement ok
COMMIT;

# 10, not 15: the five rows inserted in the same transaction were not visible to the scan.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mf_ryw_copy;
----
10

# ⚠ THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THE 10 MEANINGFUL. Without it, a `10` above would pass equally if the
# INSERT had simply done nothing — which is exactly the pre-fix failure mode.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mf_ryw;
----
15

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §5  MARS OFF + `read_isolation` — the combination that was REFUSED outright
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `mssql_materialize=false` + `mssql_read_isolation` is refused as contradictory: one asks for the
# scan to stay OUTSIDE the transaction on a pooled connection, the other for every read to be INSIDE
# it. Both are active requests, so refusing and letting the user pick is right.
#
# ⚠ BUT THAT CHECK TESTED THE RESOLVED VALUE, AND `materialize` NOW DEFAULTS TO MARS — so on any
# no-MARS engine it resolves false with NOBODY having requested it, and the refusal fired for every
# user who set `read_isolation` alone. Measured: 3 of 3 runs refused on box with MARS off; on
# Fabric, where the default is always false, `read_isolation` would have been unusable outright.
# A DEFAULT IS NOT A REQUEST — the check now tests what the user supplied, not what resolved.

# ⚠ `mars 'false'` as an ATTACH option, NOT a SET: a SET would apply to m_on and m_off as well (it outranks
# the option and is not per catalog), which would undo §1's control and every assertion that rests on it.
statement ok
ATTACH '${MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN}' AS r_iso (TYPE fabricator, read_isolation 'snapshot', mars 'false');

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE r_iso.dbo.ri_src AS SELECT i AS a FROM range(200) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE r_iso.dbo.ri_dst AS SELECT i AS a FROM range(1) t(i) WHERE false;

statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO r_iso.dbo.ri_src VALUES (999);

# The marked scan takes the POOLED snapshot route even here: `read_isolation` pins ORDINARY reads,
# but pinning a scan that feeds a bulk load is the collision this whole file is about.
statement ok
INSERT INTO r_iso.dbo.ri_dst SELECT a FROM r_iso.dbo.ri_src;

statement ok
COMMIT;

# 200, not 201 — committed state, the same read-your-writes trade as §4.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM r_iso.dbo.ri_dst;
----
200

# ⚠ POSITIVE CONTROL: `read_isolation` MUST STILL PIN AN ORDINARY READ. Without this, §5 above would
# pass equally if the option had simply been disabled. The observable is sharp: with MARS off, reading
# a table THIS transaction has written is refused unless the read joined the transaction — so this
# SELECT succeeding is proof the pin still happens, and its count proves it sees uncommitted rows.
statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO r_iso.dbo.ri_src VALUES (1000);

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM r_iso.dbo.ri_src;
----
202

statement ok
COMMIT;

# ⚠ AND THE REFUSAL MUST SURVIVE FOR THE CASE IT WAS WRITTEN FOR — an EXPLICIT materialize=false
# beside read_isolation is still two contradicting requests. This pins that the check was NARROWED,
# not deleted.
statement ok
SET mssql_materialize='false';

statement error
INSERT INTO r_iso.dbo.ri_dst SELECT a FROM r_iso.dbo.ri_src;
----
contradict each other

statement ok
RESET mssql_materialize;

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §6  MARS OFF + `materialize='true'` — the combination the bulk deferral UNLOCKED
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Asking for the drain EXPLICITLY on a no-MARS engine used to be the broken shape: the scan pins onto
# the transaction's connection and the bulk load takes that same connection, so one of them lost —
# measured 0 of 8 runs on box, and every failing Fabric row in docs/transactions.md §5.6a.
#
# It works now because `BulkSession` WAITS FOR THE FIRST BATCH (or end-of-stream) before calling
# BulkInsert. `SqlBulkCopy.WriteToServer` holds the connection for the whole load INCLUDING the time
# it merely waits for rows, so starting it eagerly meant the load held the connection before the scan
# had asked for one. Deferring makes the order deterministic — the load always acquires SECOND, after
# a drained scan has finished and released.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS A SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION, NOT AN ACCIDENT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS PINNED. It is the only way
# to get read-your-writes on an engine without MARS. Nothing else in this file covers it: §2–§4 pin
# the DEFAULT (materialize follows MARS ⇒ false here), so a change that re-broke the explicit case
# would leave every other section green.
#
# ⚠ AND §6b IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS. §6a only says "the statement ran"; a regression could make it
# run by silently falling back to a POOLED scan, which is exactly §4's behaviour. §6b is the
# discriminator: same engine, same statement, ONE setting different from §4, opposite answer.

statement ok
SET mssql_materialize='true';

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_src AS SELECT i AS a, ('v' || i::VARCHAR) AS b FROM range(200) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_dst AS SELECT i AS a, ('v' || i::VARCHAR) AS b FROM range(1) t(i) WHERE false;

# §6a — the 0-of-8 shape: an explicit drain on a no-MARS engine.
statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mt_dst SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mt_src;

query II
SELECT count(*), sum(a) FROM m_off.dbo.mt_dst;
----
200	19900

# §6b — READ-YOUR-WRITES IS BACK. Contrast with §4, which is byte-identical apart from this SET and
# answers 10: there the scan is pooled and sees committed state only; here it is pinned inside the
# transaction and sees the five rows this transaction has not committed yet.
statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_ryw AS SELECT i AS a FROM range(10) t(i);

statement ok
BEGIN;

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mt_ryw SELECT 100 + i FROM range(5) t(i);

statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_ryw_copy AS SELECT a FROM m_off.dbo.mt_ryw;

statement ok
COMMIT;

# 15, where §4 gives 10.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mt_ryw_copy;
----
15

# ⚠ THE EMPTY-SOURCE CASE, HERE BECAUSE THE DEFERRAL IS WHAT COULD BREAK IT. The consumer waits for
# "a batch OR completion"; waiting for a batch alone would hang forever on a source that yields none,
# and this shape still has to CREATE the table. A hang, not a wrong answer, is the failure mode — so
# this assertion is really a timeout guard.
statement ok
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_empty AS SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mt_src WHERE false;

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mt_empty;
----
0

statement ok
INSERT INTO m_off.dbo.mt_empty SELECT a, b FROM m_off.dbo.mt_src WHERE false;

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM m_off.dbo.mt_empty;
----
0

statement ok
RESET mssql_materialize;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_empty;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_ryw_copy;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_ryw;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_dst;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mt_src;

statement ok
DROP TABLE r_iso.dbo.ri_dst;

statement ok
DROP TABLE r_iso.dbo.ri_src;

statement ok
DETACH r_iso;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ryw_copy;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ryw;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ctas2;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_ctas;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_dst;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_off.dbo.mf_src;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_ryw_copy;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_ryw;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_dst;

statement ok
DROP TABLE m_on.dbo.mo_src;

statement ok
DETACH m_off;

statement ok
DETACH m_on;
